globalThis.DominanceComputerContent={"version":1,"course":"Computer Appreciation","scope":"HB1:First","status":"academic-review-pending","sources":[{"id":"CSM177-LECTURER","title":"Computer Appreciation — CSM 177 Lecturer Notes.pdf","url":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1H-YGfGi2LrjUy7cNo0zFsbaIKy42IBSz/view","pages":542,"sha256":"524055ed4568892581fb7a5fe8f545f155901d3101d28b420d6f4b331e47d17c","description":"CSM 177 HB1 — Microsoft Excel and PowerPoint 2016. Supplied lecturer notes; mixed legacy interface examples. Current official syllabus not supplied."}],"blocks":[{"id":"spreadsheet-foundations","title":"Spreadsheet Foundations","lessons":["CA01","CA02","CA03"]},{"id":"spreadsheet-functions","title":"Spreadsheet Functions","lessons":["CA04","CA05","CA06"]},{"id":"data-and-charts","title":"Data Handling & Charts","lessons":["CA07","CA08","CA09"]},{"id":"presentations","title":"Presentations","lessons":["CA10","CA11","CA12"]}],"lessons":[{"id":"CA01","title":"Spreadsheets and dependable data entry","pages":[[3,15],[21,39],[102,102]],"source":"CSM177-LECTURER","markdown":"# Spreadsheets and dependable data entry\n\n## Learning goals\nExplain what a spreadsheet does, distinguish text from numbers, and edit a value deliberately.\n\n## From a worksheet to a calculation\nA spreadsheet arranges data in rows and columns. Their intersection is a cell. A worksheet is a sheet of cells; a workbook holds worksheets. Use a spreadsheet to store, compare and calculate data, then communicate results with charts. The lecturer notes describe financial planning, scientific calculations and hospital data as applications. Software performs the calculation you specify; you still need to check the inputs and their meaning.\n\n## Enter the right kind of value\nEnter quantities as numbers when you intend to calculate with them. Text labels describe those quantities. A fictional equipment register might have an item name in A2, quantity 4 in B2 and unit price 12 in C2. Keep units in a heading such as “Price (GHS)” rather than typing “12 cedis” into each numeric cell.\nAn identifier is different from a quantity. Enter an apostrophe before a numeric-looking identifier, such as '0017, to preserve it as text. The apostrophe marks the entry as text rather than becoming part of the displayed identifier. Do not assume that everything made of digits should be added.\n\n## Edit and check\nSelect a cell to inspect its contents in the formula bar. Double-click a cell to edit it; Enter accepts the change and Escape cancels the edit. Clear an unwanted entry with Delete or Clear Contents rather than replacing it with a space. Clearing contents leaves the cell in place, whereas deleting a cell can move other cells.\n\n## Try it\nCreate three fictional equipment rows with numeric quantities and prices. Correct one quantity, cancel a second edit, then clear one entry. Explain why the item identifier should stay text. This is a practice exercise, not real patient data.","attribution":"CSM 177 HB1 — Microsoft Excel and PowerPoint 2016 lecturer notes. Adapted review candidate.","references":["PDF pp. 3–15","PDF pp. 21–39","PDF pp. 102"]},{"id":"CA02","title":"Editing, AutoComplete and AutoFill","pages":[[37,74]],"source":"CSM177-LECTURER","markdown":"# Editing, AutoComplete and AutoFill\n\n## Learning goals\nChoose between clearing and deleting, recognise AutoComplete, and use AutoFill without assuming an unintended pattern.\n\n## Clearing changes contents; deletion changes the grid\nClear Contents removes the value or formula while leaving the cell and its formatting in place. Deleting cells removes them and shifts surrounding cells according to the chosen direction. Deleting an entire row removes that row. Before deleting, consider whether other entries must move together.\n\n## Two different time savers\nAutoComplete suggests an existing text entry from the same column as you type its opening characters. Accept the suggestion with Enter or continue typing a different entry. It is useful for repeated labels; it is not a general method for generating numeric sequences.\nAutoFill uses the fill handle at a selection's edge to copy data, extend a detected series or fill formulas into adjacent cells. Selecting 2 and 4 as the first two entries establishes a difference of 2; extending that series produces 6, 8 and 10. A single number alone may be copied, so inspect the result and the Auto Fill Options rather than assuming that Excel knows your intention.\n\n## Copying formulas is not copying their displayed answers\nWhen a formula is filled, relative references adjust to its new position. A repeated formula may therefore produce different results on different rows. The next lesson explains when a reference should adjust and when it should remain fixed.\nCtrl+D fills down from the entry above; Ctrl+R fills right from the entry to the left. Both can overwrite existing values. Check the destination range first and use Undo if the fill is wrong.\n\n## Try it\nEnter Monday and Tuesday, then extend a short series. Repeat with 2 and 4. Finally copy a text label. Describe why the series and copied label behave differently.","attribution":"CSM 177 HB1 — Microsoft Excel and PowerPoint 2016 lecturer notes. Adapted review candidate.","references":["PDF pp. 37–74"]},{"id":"CA03","title":"Formulas, references and evaluation order","pages":[[75,117],[118,126]],"source":"CSM177-LECTURER","markdown":"# Formulas, references and evaluation order\n\n## Learning goals\nWrite a formula, predict a copied reference, and explain why parentheses change a result.\n\n## Build a calculation\nUse = to begin formulas in these lessons. With quantity in B2 and unit price in C2, =B2*C2 calculates the row cost. The cell normally displays the result; the formula bar shows the formula. Changing an input recalculates dependent formulas under normal automatic calculation.\nMultiplication uses *, division / and powers ^. Multiplication and division have equal precedence and are evaluated left to right; addition and subtraction also share a precedence level. Parentheses make the intended grouping explicit. Thus =6+27/3 gives 15, while =(6+27)/3 gives 11. =10/5*2 gives 4, whereas =10/(5*2) gives 1.\n\n## Decide which references may move\n| Reference | Fixed part | When copied one column right and one row down |\n|---|---|---|\n| B2 | Neither | C3 |\n| $B$2 | Column and row | $B$2 |\n| $B2 | Column | $B3 |\n| B$2 | Row | C$2 |\nFor a shared rate in F1, =B2*$F$1 copied down becomes =B3*$F$1. Only the row-specific quantity moves. When editing a reference, F4 cycles through reference types on supported keyboards.\n\n## Read and diagnose formulas\nA colon denotes an inclusive range: B2:B5 includes four cells. An exclamation mark separates a sheet name from a cell, as in ='Stock List'!B2. An ampersand joins text, as in =A2&\" \"&B2. A formula in D3 that refers to D3 depends on itself and creates a circular reference. Check the intended input range rather than treating that as ordinary addressing.\n\n## Try it\nPredict =B2*$F$1 after copying one row down. Then compute the four evaluation-order examples before checking them in Excel.","attribution":"CSM 177 HB1 — Microsoft Excel and PowerPoint 2016 lecturer notes. Adapted review candidate.","references":["PDF pp. 75–117","PDF pp. 118–126"]},{"id":"CA04","title":"Functions, dates and common calculations","pages":[[127,167]],"source":"CSM177-LECTURER","markdown":"# Functions, dates and common calculations\n\n## Learning goals\nRead function syntax, choose a simple calculation, and distinguish a live date function from a typed date.\n\n## A function is a named calculation\nA function has a name followed by parentheses containing its arguments. An argument can be a number, text, a reference or a range, depending on the function. =SUM(B2:B5) adds the four referenced cells. Inspect the selected range when using AutoSum; a suggested range still needs checking. These examples use commas as separators; local Excel settings may use a different separator.\n\n## Calculate and explain\n| Formula | Result | Reason |\n|---|---|---|\n| =SUM(4,6,10) | 20 | Adds the arguments |\n| =ABS(-4.5) | 4.5 | Returns magnitude without the sign |\n| =MOD(17,5) | 2 | Remainder after division |\n| =SQRT(81) | 9 | Positive square root |\n| =INT(3.8) | 3 | Rounds down to an integer |\n| =ROUNDUP(1.234,2) | 1.24 | Rounds away from zero to two decimal places |\nA function's result must answer the intended question. A sum describes a total; a remainder describes what is left after whole groups. They are not interchangeable just because both accept numbers.\n\n## Dates are values too\nExcel represents dates with serial values and times as fractions of a day, allowing date/time calculations. Use =DATE(2026,10,19) to construct an unambiguous date from year, month and day arguments. TODAY() returns the current date, while NOW() returns the current date and time. Both have empty argument lists and can change when the workbook recalculates; they are unsuitable as a permanent record of the day an event originally occurred.\n\n## Try it\nCalculate the total of 4, 6 and 10. Divide 17 items into groups of five and explain both the whole-group count and the remainder. Compare TODAY() with a deliberately entered fixed date.","attribution":"CSM 177 HB1 — Microsoft Excel and PowerPoint 2016 lecturer notes. Adapted review candidate.","references":["PDF pp. 127–167"]},{"id":"CA05","title":"Describing and counting numeric data","pages":[[168,177]],"source":"CSM177-LECTURER","markdown":"# Describing and counting numeric data\n\n## Learning goals\nDistinguish totals, averages and counts, and interpret simple descriptive statistics.\n\n## Choose the question before the function\nSuppose a fictional practice dataset contains 2, 4 and 9. SUM gives 15; AVERAGE gives an arithmetic mean of 5. Neither is a count: there are three observations. For 2, 4 and 9 the median is 4, the middle value after ordering. A mode describes a most frequent value; the set 2, 2, 9 has mode 2. A mean and median answer different questions even when they sometimes happen to match.\n\n## Count what you intend to count\nCOUNT counts numeric entries in a referenced range. COUNTA counts nonempty entries, including text. If A2:A5 contains numeric 10, numeric 12, text “pending” and one genuinely empty cell, COUNT(A2:A5) gives 2 and COUNTA(A2:A5) gives 3. A text label is evidence of an entry, not a numeric measurement.\nCOUNTIF applies one criterion. For numeric values 35, 40, 55 and 40 in B2:B5, =COUNTIF(B2:B5,\">=40\") gives 3. The comparison operator is part of the quoted criterion. State the boundary explicitly: >=40 includes 40; >40 excludes it.\n\n## Spread and selection\nMIN and MAX return the smallest and largest numeric values. SMALL and LARGE select the k-th smallest or largest. The lecturer notes distinguish sample standard deviation, STDEV, from whole-population standard deviation, STDEVP. Choose the population or sample interpretation from the study design; a spreadsheet does not make that decision for you. These legacy function names appear in the supplied notes.\n\n## Try it\nFor 2, 4 and 9, calculate the total, mean, median and count by hand. Then add the text label “pending” in another cell and explain why it should not be interpreted as a fourth numeric observation.","attribution":"CSM 177 HB1 — Microsoft Excel and PowerPoint 2016 lecturer notes. Adapted review candidate.","references":["PDF pp. 168–177"]},{"id":"CA06","title":"Text, logical tests and financial arguments","pages":[[178,203],[222,223]],"source":"CSM177-LECTURER","markdown":"# Text, logical tests and financial arguments\n\n## Learning goals\nTransform text deliberately, trace IF/AND/OR, and keep financial periods consistent.\n\n## Text operations have a purpose\nThe & operator and CONCATENATE join text. =\"HB\"&1 produces HB1. LEN counts characters, LOWER changes letters to lowercase and UPPER changes them to uppercase. LEFT and RIGHT extract characters from the start and end of text. These functions return transformed text; they do not correct the underlying meaning of an identifier or name. Preserve an original column when checking transformations.\n\n## Make a decision explicit\nIF has a logical test, a value for a true test and a value for a false test. With a fictional practice score in B2, =IF(B2>=50,\"Review complete\",\"Keep practising\") returns the first label when B2 is 50 or more, and the second otherwise. This is an exercise threshold, not an institutional pass rule.\nAND is true only when all its conditions are true. OR is true when at least one is true. For C1=10, D1=16 and E1=20, =AND(C1>D1,E1>10) is FALSE while =OR(C1>D1,E1>10) is TRUE. Trace each condition before combining them. At B2=50, a >=50 test is true but a >50 test is false.\n\n## Financial functions require consistent periods\nThe supplied financial section introduces PV (present value), FV (future value), PMT (periodic payment), rate and nper (number of periods). For a five-year loan with monthly payments, nper is 60. If an exercise specifies a nominal annual rate of 12% divided monthly, its period rate is 12%/12. Do not combine a monthly rate with five yearly periods. Cash-flow signs and payment timing also matter; this lesson does not turn an exercise into financial advice.\n\n## Try it\nTrace both logical formulas above without Excel. Then state the rate and nper for the specified five-year monthly exercise before choosing any financial function.","attribution":"CSM 177 HB1 — Microsoft Excel and PowerPoint 2016 lecturer notes. Adapted review candidate.","references":["PDF pp. 178–203","PDF pp. 222–223"]},{"id":"CA07","title":"Formatting, printing and workbook care","pages":[[204,221],[224,309]],"source":"CSM177-LECTURER","markdown":"# Formatting, printing and workbook care\n\n## Learning goals\nSeparate appearance from stored values, prepare a readable printout, and save a workbook deliberately.\n\n## Format the display, preserve the value\nNumber formats control how values appear. They do not normally change the underlying number used in a calculation. A stored value of 0.25 displayed as Percentage becomes 25%; it is still 0.25. Applying Percentage to the stored value 25 displays 2500%, a clue to inspect the input rather than merely changing decimal places. Currency formatting adds a monetary display convention; it does not perform a currency conversion.\nWrap Text makes a long entry appear on multiple lines within a cell. Adjust widths and row heights so headings and data remain readable. Format Painter copies formatting to another selection. These actions serve different purposes from editing a value or copying a formula.\n\n## Check the printed result\nUse Print Preview before printing. Page orientation, margins, scaling, print area, and headers/footers affect what reaches the page. Landscape may help a wide table, but forcing a very large sheet onto one page can make its text too small. A print area selects the range intended for printing; it does not delete data outside that range. Check all preview pages for cut-off headings and unwanted blank pages.\n\n## Save and organise\nSave updates the current file. Save As lets you choose a new name, location or file type. Save a copy before experimenting with major changes. Rename worksheets so their purpose is clear and verify which sheet is active before editing or deleting it. Workbook structure protection concerns operations such as adding sheets, whereas sheet protection concerns permitted edits within a sheet. File encryption is a separate option; do not treat every protection command as equivalent.\n\n## Try it\nFormat 0.25 as a percentage and inspect its value in the formula bar. Prepare a print area for a small fictional table, preview it, and save a separately named practice copy.","attribution":"CSM 177 HB1 — Microsoft Excel and PowerPoint 2016 lecturer notes. Adapted review candidate.","references":["PDF pp. 204–221","PDF pp. 224–309"]},{"id":"CA08","title":"Choosing and checking charts","pages":[[312,376]],"source":"CSM177-LECTURER","markdown":"# Choosing and checking charts\n\n## Learning goals\nMatch a chart to a question and verify that labels and plotted values mean what you intend.\n\n## Start with data and the comparison\nA chart represents selected worksheet data. A data series is a collection of related data points; categories describe the groups or labels. Select the intended data and headings, insert an appropriate chart, then inspect the resulting axes, legend and series. A chart can look plausible while using the wrong range.\n\n## Use the relationship to choose the chart\n| Task | Useful starting point | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| Compare totals for three departments | Column or bar chart | Compares distinct categories |\n| Show weekly counts in chronological order | Line chart | Shows a sequence across time |\n| Show one total divided into a few parts | Pie chart | Shows contributions to a whole |\n| Compare two numeric measurements for each observation | XY scatter chart | Uses numeric x and y values |\nA scatter chart is especially useful when x values are unevenly spaced, such as measurements at days 1, 3 and 10. Place corresponding x and y values in paired columns. A line chart's category positions and a scatter chart's numeric x positions are not the same idea.\n\n## Read before presenting\nCheck a chart title against its actual selection. Verify units, category labels and whether the legend names the right series. A pie chart uses one series representing a whole; it is a poor choice for several unrelated numeric relationships. Source-linked charts can update when worksheet values change, so recheck a chart after changing its source cells. Saving the workbook preserves an embedded chart with its worksheet.\n\n## Try it\nUse fictional counts for three departments to create a column chart. Then use paired day/count observations to create a scatter chart. Explain which variable belongs on each axis and why the two charts answer different questions.","attribution":"CSM 177 HB1 — Microsoft Excel and PowerPoint 2016 lecturer notes. Adapted review candidate.","references":["PDF pp. 312–376"]},{"id":"CA09","title":"Sorting and filtering records","pages":[[377,400]],"source":"CSM177-LECTURER","markdown":"# Sorting and filtering records\n\n## Learning goals\nPreserve whole records during a sort and distinguish reordering from selecting records for display.\n\n## Understand the table\nIn a rectangular data table, each row represents a record and each column a field. A fictional attendance table might have an identifier, group and attendance count on each row. Those entries describe one record and must stay together. Use one clear header row and inspect the selected range before applying a sort.\n\n## Sorting changes order\nSorting arranges records in ascending or descending order by one or more fields. To order attendance counts from highest to lowest, sort the complete table by the count field in descending order. Sorting only that column separately from its identifiers can break the relationship between them. For a two-level sort, a primary field controls the broad groups and a secondary field orders records within ties. For example, sort by group first and then by attendance count within each group.\n\n## Filtering changes which rows are visible\nA filter displays records meeting a criterion. It does not need to rearrange them or delete excluded records. A numeric filter >=40 includes values equal to 40; >40 does not. Clearing a filter restores the display of rows that the filter hid. Check whether a filter is active before deciding that records have disappeared.\nFilters on multiple columns narrow a selection using the specified conditions. State the desired rule in words first: “Group A records with a count of at least 40.” Then apply the group and numeric criteria and inspect a few boundary examples.\n\n## Try it\nMake four fictional records with counts 35, 40, 55 and 40. Filter for >=40 and predict three visible records. Clear the filter, sort the complete table in descending count order, and verify that every identifier still belongs to the correct count.","attribution":"CSM 177 HB1 — Microsoft Excel and PowerPoint 2016 lecturer notes. Adapted review candidate.","references":["PDF pp. 377–400"]},{"id":"CA10","title":"PowerPoint slides, layouts and saved files","pages":[[401,456]],"source":"CSM177-LECTURER","markdown":"# PowerPoint slides, layouts and saved files\n\n## Learning goals\nDistinguish a presentation, layout and theme, then create and save a short deck.\n\n## Build a presentation from slides\nPowerPoint is presentation software. A presentation contains slides; each slide communicates part of the overall explanation. Start with a blank presentation or a template, then add the slides you need. A template is a starting document; review its existing content before using it.\nA layout arranges placeholders for a slide's content. A Title and Content layout provides a title placeholder and an area for content such as text, tables or pictures. Changing a layout changes the arrangement; it does not automatically supply academically correct content. Insert, copy, reorder or delete slides deliberately and check that the resulting sequence still makes sense.\n\n## Separate arrangement from appearance\nA theme is a predefined combination of colours, fonts and effects that can be applied to a presentation. It promotes visual consistency across slides. Layout answers “where does content go?” while theme answers “what coordinated appearance does it use?” Text boxes add text beyond a layout's placeholders. Bullets present parallel points; numbered lists make order explicit.\n\n## Save a usable file\nSave As selects a name, location and format; subsequent Save updates the current file. The .pptx format keeps a normal editable presentation. A .ppsx PowerPoint Show is intended to open in Slide Show view. A PDF is useful for a fixed-layout copy, but is not a substitute for the editable deck when someone needs to revise slides. Check the saved file and keep the working presentation.\n\n## Try it\nCreate a three-slide explanation of a fictional study routine: purpose, steps and recap. Choose layouts to fit each slide, apply one theme, save as .pptx and reopen it. Confirm that the file you reopened is the editable presentation.","attribution":"CSM 177 HB1 — Microsoft Excel and PowerPoint 2016 lecturer notes. Adapted review candidate.","references":["PDF pp. 401–456"]},{"id":"CA11","title":"Tables, pictures and presentation objects","pages":[[457,511]],"source":"CSM177-LECTURER","markdown":"# Tables, pictures and presentation objects\n\n## Learning goals\nChoose an object that communicates the information, then align, group and order objects intentionally.\n\n## Use the right object\nA table organises information into rows and columns. Insert a table, enter a short comparison and add or remove rows and columns when needed. A picture can communicate a visual idea; insert the intended file and inspect its size and placement. Resizing handles change size, whereas dragging the object changes its position. Keep labels associated with the correct visual.\nSmartArt provides structured diagram layouts and can turn a list into a diagram. Choose a structure that matches the relationship you want to explain. A diagram format does not prove that its labels or relationships are accurate.\n\n## Position related objects\nAlignment places objects along a common edge or centre. The reference matters: Align Top relative to the slide moves objects toward the slide's top; Align Top relative to selected objects aligns their tops with each other. Grouping treats selected objects as a unit so they can move together. Ungroup when individual edits are needed.\nObjects also have a stacking order. Bring to Front puts an object above the others; Send to Back puts it behind the entire stack. Send Backward moves it back one layer. If a picture covers a label, inspect stacking order before deleting either object.\n\n## Check multimedia in context\nThe notes introduce inserting audio and video, sizing a media object and choosing playback options. Test media in Slide Show view on the computer intended for presenting. Confirm that the chosen file plays, the sound can be heard and the timing fits the explanation. Menu locations differ across Office versions; this lesson teaches the task rather than promising identical commands everywhere.\n\n## Try it\nPlace two labelled shapes on a slide. Align their tops, group and move them, then ungroup. Put one shape behind the other and explain the difference between Send Backward and Send to Back.","attribution":"CSM 177 HB1 — Microsoft Excel and PowerPoint 2016 lecturer notes. Adapted review candidate.","references":["PDF pp. 457–511"]},{"id":"CA12","title":"Presenting, printing and controlled effects","pages":[[512,542]],"source":"CSM177-LECTURER","markdown":"# Presenting, printing and controlled effects\n\n## Learning goals\nChoose an appropriate view, distinguish animations from transitions, and rehearse the saved presentation.\n\n## Choose a view for the task\nNormal view is for creating and editing slide content. Slide Sorter shows slide thumbnails, making it useful for checking the overall order and dragging slides into a new sequence. Slide Show presents the slides as the audience sees them. Inspecting a deck in editing view alone does not reveal every playback or timing problem.\nIn Slide Show, navigation keys such as the arrows, Page Down and Enter move through slides; Escape ends the show. Rehearse the sequence and test how each slide advances rather than assuming that it waits for a click.\n\n## Animation is within a slide; transition is between slides\nAn animation acts on text or another object on a slide. A transition affects the change from one slide to the next. To reveal three points one at a time, work with object/text animations. To apply an effect when a new slide appears, use a transition. Review order and timing after changing effects. Applying an effect to all slides should be an intentional choice.\nThe lecturer notes mix commands from older Office interfaces with Office 2016 terminology. Use the Animations controls for object effects and the Transitions controls for between-slide effects in current ribbon interfaces. The conceptual distinction is the assessed objective, not a memorised legacy tab location.\n\n## Check delivery and printed output\nUse Print Preview to choose the intended print output, page range, colour mode and number of copies. Handouts support an audience; the editable deck supports further revision. Preview every selected page rather than assuming the on-screen layout will print identically.\nReopen the saved presentation, use Slide Sorter to check order, run Slide Show, test media and transitions, and inspect the print preview. These checks validate delivery, not the academic truth of slide content.\n\n## Try it\nRehearse a three-slide deck. Add one object animation and one between-slide transition, identify each, and confirm that Escape exits the show. Remove any effect that gets in the way of the explanation.","attribution":"CSM 177 HB1 — Microsoft Excel and PowerPoint 2016 lecturer notes. Adapted review candidate.","references":["PDF pp. 512–542"]}],"questions":[{"id":"CA-Q001","lesson":"CA01","kind":"foundation","question":"What is a cell in a spreadsheet?","options":["The intersection of a row and a column","An entire row of entries","An entire column of entries","A collection of worksheets"],"letter":"A","explanation":"A cell is one location where a row and a column meet. A workbook contains sheets, while a legend describes chart series.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[5,5]]},{"id":"CA-Q002","lesson":"CA01","kind":"foundation","question":"Which distinction between a workbook and a worksheet is correct?","options":["A worksheet contains all workbooks in a folder","A workbook can contain worksheets","A workbook is always one individual cell","A workbook is another name for a formula"],"letter":"B","explanation":"A worksheet is a sheet of cells, and a workbook holds worksheets. A value or formula belongs within a cell.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[102,102]]},{"id":"CA-Q003","lesson":"CA01","kind":"foundation","question":"What does pressing Escape while editing a cell normally do?","options":["Accepts the current edit","Clears the cell after accepting the edit","Cancels the current edit","Reverts all changes since the file was opened"],"letter":"C","explanation":"Escape abandons the edit in progress. Enter accepts it; neither key is a command to sort or delete a worksheet.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[22,22]]},{"id":"CA-Q004","lesson":"CA01","kind":"application","question":"An equipment identifier is 0017 and must keep its leading zeros as text. Which entry fits that purpose?","options":["=17+0","17","0017*1","'0017"],"letter":"D","explanation":"An initial apostrophe explicitly marks a numeric-looking entry as text. Arithmetic would treat the identifier as a quantity and does not preserve its intended text representation.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[24,24]]},{"id":"CA-Q005","lesson":"CA01","kind":"application","question":"Which pair is best for calculating an equipment cost later?","options":["Quantity 4 and unit price 12 in separate numeric cells","Quantity and price together as the text 4 items at 12 cedis","Quantity four and price twelve entered as words","A numeric quantity and price entered as the text pending"],"letter":"A","explanation":"Separate numeric inputs can be referenced in a multiplication formula. Words and combined descriptive text are not equivalent to the required numeric inputs.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[5,5]]},{"id":"CA-Q006","lesson":"CA01","kind":"application","question":"A wrong value must be removed while its cell and format remain. Which action fits?","options":["Delete the entire row","Clear Contents","Replace the value with a space","Delete the cell and shift surrounding cells up"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Clear Contents removes the entry without shifting the cell. A space is still text, and deleting a row or worksheet removes more than the unwanted value.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[39,39]]},{"id":"CA-Q007","lesson":"CA02","kind":"foundation","question":"What does AutoComplete primarily help with in a column?","options":["Extending a numeric series from two seed values","Adjusting relative references in copied formulas","Repeating existing text entries","Copying a selected cell format to other cells"],"letter":"C","explanation":"AutoComplete suggests text based on existing entries in the same column. It does not perform security, statistical-design or presentation tasks.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[46,46]]},{"id":"CA-Q008","lesson":"CA02","kind":"foundation","question":"What is the fill handle used for?","options":["Only changing the display number format","Only suggesting an existing text label while typing","Only renaming the selected cells","Copying entries or extending a series into adjacent cells"],"letter":"D","explanation":"Dragging the fill handle can copy data, extend series or fill formulas. Inspect the result and fill options because the intended operation is not always automatic.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[49,49]]},{"id":"CA-Q009","lesson":"CA02","kind":"foundation","question":"Which keyboard shortcut fills from the cell above?","options":["Ctrl+D","Ctrl+R","Ctrl+Z","Ctrl+V"],"letter":"A","explanation":"Ctrl+D fills down. Ctrl+R fills right, Ctrl+Z undoes an action and Ctrl+P opens printing.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[56,56]]},{"id":"CA-Q010","lesson":"CA02","kind":"application","question":"Cells A1 and A2 contain 2 and 4. Both are selected and their arithmetic series is extended two cells down. What follows?","options":["4 and 4","6 and 8","8 and 16","5 and 6"],"letter":"B","explanation":"The selected entries establish a step of 2. Extending that arithmetic series adds 2 each time; it does not double each previous value.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[61,61]]},{"id":"CA-Q011","lesson":"CA02","kind":"application","question":"A table row must be removed and the lower rows must move up together. Which action is appropriate?","options":["Clear the contents of one cell in the row","Clear all row contents but leave the row in place","Delete the entire row","Hide the row without deleting it"],"letter":"C","explanation":"Deleting the entire row shifts the rows beneath it together. Clearing one cell leaves the row in place; formatting does not remove it.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[40,40]]},{"id":"CA-Q012","lesson":"CA02","kind":"application","question":"Dragging the fill handle overwrote valid destination entries. What should you do first?","options":["Save the overwritten cells before checking","Clear the destination and assume the old values return","Change the destination number format to recover the old values","Undo and check the destination range before repeating"],"letter":"D","explanation":"Undo can reverse an unintended fill. Checking the target range prevents repeating the overwrite; a visual formatting change cannot recover the original entries.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[51,51]]},{"id":"CA-Q013","lesson":"CA03","kind":"foundation","question":"Which reference fixes both column and row when copied?","options":["$B$2","B2","$B2","B$2"],"letter":"A","explanation":"Dollar signs before both B and 2 fix both components. $B2 fixes only the column; B$2 fixes only the row.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[97,97]]},{"id":"CA-Q014","lesson":"CA03","kind":"foundation","question":"What does B2:B5 denote?","options":["Only the two endpoint cells B2 and B5","The inclusive range from B2 through B5","The value in B2 divided by B5","Text formed by joining B2 and B5"],"letter":"B","explanation":"The colon is a range operator and includes the endpoints and cells between them. It is neither division nor text concatenation.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[110,110]]},{"id":"CA-Q015","lesson":"CA03","kind":"foundation","question":"A formula in D3 includes D3 as an input. What problem does this create?","options":["A reference with both column and row fixed","A reference to another worksheet","A circular reference","A reference to all cells between two addresses"],"letter":"C","explanation":"The formula depends on its own result. That is a circular reference, distinct from fixing a reference with dollar signs.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[101,101]]},{"id":"CA-Q016","lesson":"CA03","kind":"application","question":"What does =6+27/3 evaluate to?","options":["11","33","9","15"],"letter":"D","explanation":"Division is evaluated before addition: 27/3 is 9, then 6+9 is 15. Parentheses around 6+27 would produce 11 instead.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[112,112]]},{"id":"CA-Q017","lesson":"CA03","kind":"application","question":"The formula =B2*$F$1 is copied from D2 to D3. What is the copied formula?","options":["=B3*$F$1","=B2*$F$1","=B3*$F$2","=C2*$G$1"],"letter":"A","explanation":"The copy moves one row down. Relative B2 becomes B3; both parts of $F$1 remain fixed.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[97,97]]},{"id":"CA-Q018","lesson":"CA03","kind":"application","question":"The reference $B2 is copied one column right and one row down. What does it become?","options":["$C3","$B3","$B2","C$2"],"letter":"B","explanation":"The column B is absolute and stays B. The relative row moves from 2 to 3, giving $B3.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[99,99]]},{"id":"CA-Q019","lesson":"CA04","kind":"foundation","question":"Which function adds its numeric arguments?","options":["MOD","SQRT","SUM","ABS"],"letter":"C","explanation":"SUM totals values. MOD returns a remainder, SQRT a square root and ABS an absolute value.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[166,166]]},{"id":"CA-Q020","lesson":"CA04","kind":"foundation","question":"Which function returns the current date and time when recalculated?","options":["TODAY()","DATE(2026,10,19)","TIME(12,0,0)","NOW()"],"letter":"D","explanation":"NOW returns a date/time value. TODAY returns the date, while DATE constructs a specified date from its arguments.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[153,153]]},{"id":"CA-Q021","lesson":"CA04","kind":"foundation","question":"What is the argument order in DATE?","options":["Year, month, day","Day, month, year","Month, day, year","Year, day, month"],"letter":"A","explanation":"DATE takes year, month and day. Writing the arguments explicitly avoids relying on an ambiguous typed date string.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[156,156]]},{"id":"CA-Q022","lesson":"CA04","kind":"application","question":"What is =MOD(17,5)?","options":["3","2","3.4","5"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Three whole groups of 5 use 15 of the 17 items, leaving a remainder of 2. The quotient is a different calculation.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[164,164]]},{"id":"CA-Q023","lesson":"CA04","kind":"application","question":"What is =SQRT(81)?","options":["40.5","6561","9","-81"],"letter":"C","explanation":"SQRT returns the positive square root: 9 multiplied by 9 is 81. Squaring 81 would instead give 6561.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[166,166]]},{"id":"CA-Q024","lesson":"CA04","kind":"application","question":"A workbook must preserve a fixed event date rather than show the day it is reopened. Which choice fits?","options":["Use TODAY() and assume it never recalculates","Use NOW() and assume its first result is permanent","Use a date format alone without entering any value","Enter the intended fixed date"],"letter":"D","explanation":"TODAY and NOW can change on recalculation. A deliberately stored fixed date preserves the intended event date instead of following the current clock.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[154,154]]},{"id":"CA-Q025","lesson":"CA05","kind":"foundation","question":"Which function counts numeric cells in a referenced range?","options":["COUNT","COUNTA","COUNTIF with a criterion of nonempty","SUM"],"letter":"A","explanation":"COUNT counts numeric entries; COUNTA counts nonempty entries more broadly. Text functions do not count numeric observations.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[170,170]]},{"id":"CA-Q026","lesson":"CA05","kind":"foundation","question":"What does the median represent?","options":["The largest value in the observations","The middle value after ordering the observations","The arithmetic mean of the observations","The most frequent observed value"],"letter":"B","explanation":"The median is based on ordered position. A mode is a most frequent value, and a sum is a total.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[173,173]]},{"id":"CA-Q027","lesson":"CA05","kind":"foundation","question":"In the lecturer notes, STDEV estimates spread for which interpretation?","options":["The entire population","The mean alone, without measuring spread","A sample","The count of nonempty observations"],"letter":"C","explanation":"The notes distinguish sample standard deviation (STDEV) from population standard deviation (STDEVP). The appropriate interpretation depends on the dataset.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[175,175]]},{"id":"CA-Q028","lesson":"CA05","kind":"application","question":"What is the arithmetic mean of 2, 4 and 9?","options":["4","15","3","5"],"letter":"D","explanation":"The sum is 15 and there are three values, so the arithmetic mean is 15/3 = 5. The median is 4 and the count is 3.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[168,168]]},{"id":"CA-Q029","lesson":"CA05","kind":"application","question":"A2:A5 contains numeric 10, numeric 12, text pending and one empty cell. What are COUNT and COUNTA respectively?","options":["2 and 3","3 and 3","2 and 4","4 and 2"],"letter":"A","explanation":"COUNT includes the two numeric entries. COUNTA also includes the nonempty text entry, but not the genuinely empty cell.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[170,170]]},{"id":"CA-Q030","lesson":"CA05","kind":"application","question":"B2:B5 contains 35, 40, 55 and 40. What does =COUNTIF(B2:B5,\">=40\") return?","options":["1","3","2","4"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Both 40s and 55 meet the inclusive >=40 criterion. A >40 criterion would count only 55.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[171,171]]},{"id":"CA-Q031","lesson":"CA06","kind":"foundation","question":"Which operator joins text in Excel?","options":["^","+","&","\u003c>"],"letter":"C","explanation":"The ampersand concatenates text. The caret is exponentiation, slash division and \u003c> a not-equal comparison.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[223,223]]},{"id":"CA-Q032","lesson":"CA06","kind":"foundation","question":"When does AND return TRUE?","options":["Whenever any one condition is TRUE","Only when every condition is FALSE","When exactly one condition is TRUE","When every condition is TRUE"],"letter":"D","explanation":"AND requires all conditions to be true. OR requires at least one true condition.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[190,190]]},{"id":"CA-Q033","lesson":"CA06","kind":"foundation","question":"What does nper specify in a financial function?","options":["The number of periods","The interest rate for one period","The current present value","The size of each payment"],"letter":"A","explanation":"nper counts payment or investment periods. It must use periods consistent with the specified rate.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[181,181]]},{"id":"CA-Q034","lesson":"CA06","kind":"application","question":"B2 contains 50. What is =IF(B2>=50,\"Review complete\",\"Keep practising\")?","options":["Keep practising","Review complete","50","FALSE"],"letter":"B","explanation":"50 meets the >=50 condition, so IF returns the first text label. This exercise threshold is not an institutional pass rule.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[192,192]]},{"id":"CA-Q035","lesson":"CA06","kind":"application","question":"C1=10, D1=16 and E1=20. What is =OR(C1>D1,E1>10)?","options":["FALSE","10","TRUE","16"],"letter":"C","explanation":"10>16 is false but 20>10 is true. OR needs at least one true condition, so its result is TRUE.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[191,191]]},{"id":"CA-Q036","lesson":"CA06","kind":"application","question":"An exercise specifies five years of monthly payments and a nominal 12% annual rate divided monthly. Which pair is consistent?","options":["Rate 12%; nper 60","Rate 12%/12; nper 5","Rate 12%*12; nper 5","Rate 12%/12; nper 60"],"letter":"D","explanation":"The rate is per month, so the period count must also be monthly: 5*12 = 60. Mixing annual and monthly units gives the wrong model.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[186,186]]},{"id":"CA-Q037","lesson":"CA07","kind":"foundation","question":"What does a number format normally change?","options":["The displayed appearance of a value","The stored magnitude used by calculations","The formula references in the cell","The number of records in the table"],"letter":"A","explanation":"Formatting controls appearance while calculations use the stored value. It is separate from changing an input, password or sheet name.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[229,229]]},{"id":"CA-Q038","lesson":"CA07","kind":"foundation","question":"Which command is intended to choose a different file name or location?","options":["Save without changing the current destination","Save As","Format Painter","Rename the worksheet tab only"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Save As selects a file destination and format. The other commands affect cell presentation or contents.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[287,287]]},{"id":"CA-Q039","lesson":"CA07","kind":"foundation","question":"What is a print area?","options":["A range whose outside cells have been deleted","A range automatically converted to a chart","A selected worksheet range intended for printing","A password-restricted range of editable cells"],"letter":"C","explanation":"A print area controls the intended output range. It does not remove data outside that range or encrypt the workbook.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[269,269]]},{"id":"CA-Q040","lesson":"CA07","kind":"application","question":"The stored numeric value is 0.25. What does Percentage formatting normally display?","options":["0.25%","2500%","25 with no percent symbol","25%"],"letter":"D","explanation":"Percentage display multiplies the stored fraction by 100 and adds the percent symbol. The stored value remains 0.25.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[235,235]]},{"id":"CA-Q041","lesson":"CA07","kind":"application","question":"A long heading must display on several lines inside its cell. Which feature fits?","options":["Wrap Text","Merge unrelated records into one row","Apply Percentage format","Sort the heading alphabetically"],"letter":"A","explanation":"Wrap Text changes how content fits inside the cell. Sorting, deleting or protecting workbook structure does not wrap a heading.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[220,220]]},{"id":"CA-Q042","lesson":"CA07","kind":"application","question":"A wide table is unreadably small when forced onto one printed page. What should you inspect?","options":["Only the worksheet tab colour","Print Preview, orientation and scaling","Only the workbook name","Only the number of copies"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Preview exposes the real page layout. Orientation and scaling can change how much fits on a page; decorative colours or file names do not fix the scaling.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[263,263]]},{"id":"CA-Q043","lesson":"CA08","kind":"foundation","question":"What is a chart data series?","options":["The chart title text by itself","The boundary of the plotting rectangle","A collection of related data points","The labels defining the categories only"],"letter":"C","explanation":"A series groups related plotted values. It is distinct from worksheet security, printing margins and presentation effects.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[316,316]]},{"id":"CA-Q044","lesson":"CA08","kind":"foundation","question":"Which chart uses numeric values on both x and y axes?","options":["A line chart using equally spaced categories","Pie","Doughnut","XY scatter"],"letter":"D","explanation":"A scatter chart plots paired numeric x and y values. Pie and doughnut charts represent contributions to a whole.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[354,354]]},{"id":"CA-Q045","lesson":"CA08","kind":"foundation","question":"Which situation best fits a pie chart?","options":["One total divided into a few parts","Two numeric variables with uneven x spacing","Several unrelated numeric series at once","Values that do not represent parts of any whole"],"letter":"A","explanation":"A pie chart represents parts of one whole using a single series. It is not a general tool for paired numeric relationships.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[340,340]]},{"id":"CA-Q046","lesson":"CA08","kind":"application","question":"You want to compare totals for three departments. Which is a useful starting chart?","options":["Scatter chart with arbitrary numeric department codes as the measured x variable","Column chart","A separate pie slice for every value in several unrelated series","A line chart intended to imply a chronological department sequence"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Columns compare values for distinct categories. The departments are categories rather than paired numeric x/y observations.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[323,323]]},{"id":"CA-Q047","lesson":"CA08","kind":"application","question":"Observations were taken at days 1, 3 and 10 and numeric spacing matters. Which chart fits?","options":["Pie chart","Doughnut chart","XY scatter chart","A line chart using the day labels as equally spaced categories"],"letter":"C","explanation":"A scatter chart positions observations on a numeric x axis, preserving uneven intervals. Equally spaced categories would not preserve those distances.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[355,355]]},{"id":"CA-Q048","lesson":"CA08","kind":"application","question":"A source-linked chart looks different after the worksheet is edited. What should you check first?","options":["Only the chart font size","Only the workbook file name","Only the chart border colour","The selected source cells and chart series"],"letter":"D","explanation":"A chart linked to worksheet data can change when those inputs change. Verify its source range, labels and series before interpreting the new picture.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[370,370]]},{"id":"CA-Q049","lesson":"CA09","kind":"foundation","question":"In a rectangular data table, what does each row normally represent?","options":["A record","A field containing the same type of information across all records","A separate worksheet for each column","A heading that replaces the data below it"],"letter":"A","explanation":"A row holds related fields describing one record. The columns define the fields shared across records.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[378,378]]},{"id":"CA-Q050","lesson":"CA09","kind":"foundation","question":"What does sorting do?","options":["Displays only records meeting a criterion","Arranges records in a specified order","Deletes records below a chosen value","Converts text values into numbers"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Sorting reorders records by chosen fields. It does not inherently delete, encrypt or change their data types.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[377,377]]},{"id":"CA-Q051","lesson":"CA09","kind":"foundation","question":"What does filtering primarily change?","options":["The order of every stored record automatically","The stored values of excluded records","Which records are displayed","The existence of excluded records, which are deleted"],"letter":"C","explanation":"Filtering limits the visible rows according to criteria. Excluded records are not deleted just because they are hidden by a filter.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[392,392]]},{"id":"CA-Q052","lesson":"CA09","kind":"application","question":"Counts must be sorted while staying paired with their identifiers. What should be sorted?","options":["Only the count cells independently","Only the identifier cells independently","Only the header row","The complete table by its count field"],"letter":"D","explanation":"The fields in each record must move together. Sorting only one field can associate values with the wrong identifiers.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[378,378]]},{"id":"CA-Q053","lesson":"CA09","kind":"application","question":"Four records have counts 35, 40, 55 and 40. A >=40 filter displays how many records?","options":["3","1","2","4"],"letter":"A","explanation":"The inclusive criterion keeps both 40s and 55. Only 35 is excluded from the display.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[392,392]]},{"id":"CA-Q054","lesson":"CA09","kind":"application","question":"You need groups together and descending counts within each group. Which sort order fits?","options":["Primary count descending, with no group sort","Primary group; secondary count descending","Sort only the group column separately from its records","Filter out lower counts instead of specifying a secondary sort"],"letter":"B","explanation":"The primary sort creates the groups; the secondary sort orders tied-group records by count while keeping each record together.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[387,387]]},{"id":"CA-Q055","lesson":"CA10","kind":"foundation","question":"What does a slide layout define?","options":["The coordinated set of fonts and colours","The order of all slides in the presentation","The arrangement of content placeholders","The between-slide transition timing"],"letter":"C","explanation":"Layouts arrange placeholders for content. They cannot supply or certify its academic accuracy.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[414,414]]},{"id":"CA-Q056","lesson":"CA10","kind":"foundation","question":"What is a PowerPoint theme?","options":["The arrangement of placeholders on one slide","A list of slide titles in chronological order","An effect revealing text one object at a time","A coordinated set of colours, fonts and effects"],"letter":"D","explanation":"A theme coordinates the presentation appearance. A layout instead concerns the arrangement of content areas.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[437,437]]},{"id":"CA-Q057","lesson":"CA10","kind":"foundation","question":"Which format keeps a normal editable PowerPoint presentation?","options":[".pptx",".pdf",".csv",".jpg"],"letter":"A","explanation":"A .pptx file retains a normal editable presentation. PDF is a fixed-layout output; text and CSV do not preserve a full presentation structure.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[427,427]]},{"id":"CA-Q058","lesson":"CA10","kind":"application","question":"A slide needs a title and a main content area. Which starting layout fits?","options":["A blank layout with no placeholders","Title and Content","A title-only layout with no main content placeholder","A layout chosen only to change the colour palette"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Title and Content provides the relevant placeholders. The other choices do not define a slide arrangement.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[413,413]]},{"id":"CA-Q059","lesson":"CA10","kind":"application","question":"You want an existing deck to use a coordinated font and colour scheme. What should you apply?","options":["A transition effect","A text animation","A theme","A change to slide order"],"letter":"C","explanation":"A theme applies coordinated visual choices across slides. It does not replace the need to check individual slide content.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[437,437]]},{"id":"CA-Q060","lesson":"CA10","kind":"application","question":"A collaborator must revise slide contents later. Which file should you retain for that work?","options":["Only an image of the first slide","Only a printed handout","Only a PDF export","The editable .pptx presentation"],"letter":"D","explanation":"Keep the editable deck for revision. Exported or printed views may communicate content but do not preserve the same editing workflow.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[427,427]]},{"id":"CA-Q061","lesson":"CA11","kind":"foundation","question":"What does grouping selected slide objects allow?","options":["Moving them together as one unit","Aligning their edges without making a group","Moving each object to the back of the stack","Converting each object into a separate slide"],"letter":"A","explanation":"Grouping makes related objects easier to position together. It does not validate content or remove unrelated objects.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[490,490]]},{"id":"CA-Q062","lesson":"CA11","kind":"foundation","question":"What is SmartArt intended to help create?","options":["Between-slide transition sequences","Structured diagrams","A numerical worksheet calculation","A fixed paper print margin"],"letter":"B","explanation":"SmartArt provides diagram layouts for relationships or processes. Its layout still requires appropriate and accurate content.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[481,481]]},{"id":"CA-Q063","lesson":"CA11","kind":"foundation","question":"What does Send Backward do to a slide object?","options":["Always moves it behind the whole stack","Moves it one layer toward the front","Moves it back one layer","Moves it to the front of the whole stack"],"letter":"C","explanation":"Send Backward changes stacking by one layer. Send to Back places it behind the entire stack.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[499,499]]},{"id":"CA-Q064","lesson":"CA11","kind":"application","question":"A picture covers an important label. Which operation should you inspect?","options":["Only the picture border colour","Only the slide transition duration","Only the presentation file name","Object stacking order"],"letter":"D","explanation":"Front/back order determines which overlapping object covers another. Changing unrelated settings will not uncover the label.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[498,498]]},{"id":"CA-Q065","lesson":"CA11","kind":"application","question":"Two shapes must have their top edges aligned with each other, not the slide edge. Which reference fits?","options":["Align Selected Objects, then Align Top","Align to Slide, then Align Top","Send both shapes to Back","Align Selected Objects, then Align Bottom"],"letter":"A","explanation":"Alignment relative to selected objects uses their shared geometry. Alignment to the slide instead uses the slide boundary.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[489,489]]},{"id":"CA-Q066","lesson":"CA11","kind":"application","question":"You have inserted a video for a presentation. What is a useful delivery check?","options":["Assume the inserted icon proves playback","Play it in Slide Show on the intended presentation computer","Test only the static slide thumbnail","Check only the video object border colour"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Inserting a media object is not proof that its file, sound and timing work in the delivery environment. Test the actual presentation playback.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[503,503]]},{"id":"CA-Q067","lesson":"CA12","kind":"foundation","question":"Which view is intended for creating and editing slide contents?","options":["Slide Show view","Slide Sorter as the main text-editing surface","Normal view","Print Preview"],"letter":"C","explanation":"Normal view supports slide editing. Slide Show presents the audience view rather than the ordinary editing workspace.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[514,514]]},{"id":"CA-Q068","lesson":"CA12","kind":"foundation","question":"What distinguishes a transition from an animation?","options":["A transition acts on objects; an animation changes between slides","Both terms mean changing the order of slide thumbnails","Both terms mean changing the presentation theme","A transition acts between slides; an animation acts on objects within a slide"],"letter":"D","explanation":"Transitions concern changing slides. Animations act on text or objects on a slide; they are different operations.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[531,531]]},{"id":"CA-Q069","lesson":"CA12","kind":"foundation","question":"Which key ends a running slide show?","options":["Escape","Enter","Page Down","Right Arrow"],"letter":"A","explanation":"Escape ends Slide Show. The other keys are not the standard command for exiting a presentation show.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[515,515]]},{"id":"CA-Q070","lesson":"CA12","kind":"application","question":"You need to see many slide thumbnails and rearrange their sequence. Which view fits?","options":["Full-screen Slide Show","Slide Sorter","Normal view focused only on editing a text box","Print Preview"],"letter":"B","explanation":"Slide Sorter displays thumbnails for reviewing and changing slide order. Editing and playback views serve other main tasks.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[515,515]]},{"id":"CA-Q071","lesson":"CA12","kind":"application","question":"Three points should appear one at a time on the same slide. Which feature fits?","options":["A transition between two slides","A theme change affecting all slide fonts","Object or text animation","A new slide layout without object effects"],"letter":"C","explanation":"Revealing content within a slide is an animation task. A transition affects the change to another slide.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[520,520]]},{"id":"CA-Q072","lesson":"CA12","kind":"application","question":"Before printing audience copies, what should you check?","options":["Only the transition duration","Only the first slide title","Only the on-screen editing zoom","Print Preview and the selected output/page range"],"letter":"D","explanation":"Preview helps verify layout and the requested pages or handout output. Sound, a title and a creation date cannot establish that the intended pages will print.","source":"CSM177-LECTURER","pages":[[519,519]]}]};
